/* Notes for s29 — generated by tools/build-notes.js from content/notes. Do not edit by hand. */
PG.notes("l233", "<p class=\"summary\">Brief for the Section 29 capstone: build an Express + Axios + EJS website around a public API of the learner's choice, with error handling, comments, a README and a GitHub push. The lecture page body did not render in the browser, so this note is based on the reference repo's section README, which holds the brief.</p>\n<h3>Capstone brief</h3>\n<ul><li>Build a site on Node/Express that uses Axios to call a public API picked from the course's public-api-lists repo (or any other). Show the retrieved data in a friendly way.</li><li>Goals: practise integrating public APIs, server-side Express, client-server communication with Axios, and handling and presenting API data.</li><li>Example ideas: a joke based on the user's name (JokeAPI), a will-it-rain-tomorrow checker (OpenWeatherMap), a crypto price lookup, a random cocktail recipe with images (CocktailDB), a sunscreen-today helper (Open UV). Bonus for choosing an unusual API; if a chosen API turns out unusable, switch to another.</li></ul>\n<h3>Requirements checklist</h3>\n<ul><li>API choice: prefer one that needs no authentication and has CORS enabled.</li><li>Planning: research the API's features, the data it returns and how the app will use it before coding.</li><li>Setup: new Node project with Express, Axios and EJS, organised into a sensible directory structure (index.js, views, public).</li><li>Integration: implement at least one GET endpoint that sends Axios requests to the API and handles the responses.</li><li>Presentation: render data with EJS, HTML and CSS so it reads well for a user.</li><li>Error handling: handle failures in the app and in API calls; log errors and show user-relevant messages where useful.</li><li>Documentation: comment the code to explain the logic.</li><li>Sharing: commit and push to GitHub, and include a README explaining how to install and start (npm i, then nodemon index.js).</li></ul>\n<h3>Recommended resources</h3>\n<ul><li>Express getting-started guide, Node docs, Axios docs, the public API list repo, and RapidAPI's list of free APIs.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Typical skeleton from the reference 'Get a Joke' solution (handles a /joke route; catch block should also send a response)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nimport axios from &quot;axios&quot;;\n\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\nconst API_URL = &quot;https://v2.jokeapi.dev/joke/Any&quot;;\n\napp.use(express.static(&quot;public&quot;));\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { data: &quot;&quot; });\n});\n\napp.get(&quot;/joke&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  try {\n    const result = await axios.get(API_URL);\n    res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { data: result.data });\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.log(error.message);\n    res.status(500).send(&quot;Could not fetch a joke.&quot;);\n  }\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(`Server is running on port ${port}`);\n});</pre>\n<h2>The task: Section 29 capstone: use a public API</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Empty project; reference repo has two example solutions (29.01 Get a Joke, 29.02 Know The Rain) each with index.js, views/index.ejs and package.json.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Ship a small Express/Axios/EJS app that fetches and displays data from a public API, with error handling, comments, a README and a GitHub repo.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Browse the public API list and pick an API (no auth, CORS enabled preferred).</li><li>Read its docs and plan routes, inputs and displayed fields.</li><li>npm init, install express, axios and ejs; create index.js, views/ and public/.</li><li>Add at least one GET route that calls the API with Axios (async/await in try/catch).</li><li>Render the data via an EJS template with CSS.</li><li>Add error handling and code comments.</li><li>Write a README with run instructions and push to GitHub; optionally share in the Q&amp;A.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A working site that displays live API data in a readable layout and fails gracefully.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Picking an API that requires keys or lacks CORS/docs adds friction.</li><li>Hard-coding API keys in code pushed to GitHub.</li><li>Reference examples set res.status(500) without sending a body, leaving the request hanging.</li><li>Skipping the README or comments, which are explicit requirements.</li></ul>");
